{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1, "contents": [{}], "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n6le02ob04re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n6le02ob04re2aud01", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n6le02ob04re2img01.jpg", "text": "<strong>Susanne:</strong> Hey, Michael. Don't you think that the echo and print statements are basically the same?</br>\n<strong>Michael:</strong> Well, considering that these two statements are the basic ways to get output in PHP, and both of them can be used with or without parentheses. Yes, they are. However, they have some slight differences.</br>\n<strong>Susanne:</strong> Really? And, what are those?</br>\n<strong>Michael:</strong> I was going to get at it, but you interrupted me! </br>\n<strong>Susanne:</strong> I'm sorry! Please, continue.</br>\n<strong>Michael:</strong> Initially, the echo statement cannot return value. I think that's the biggest difference, don't you agree?</br>", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n6le02ob04re2img02.jpg", "text": "<strong>Susanne:</strong> I can't agree with you, yet. I need to understand it all first.</br>\n<strong>Michael:</strong> That's true. Anyways, other characteristics are that the echo statement can take multiple parameters.</br>\n<strong>Susanne:</strong> Wait, Don't people use rarely  echo statement?</br>\n<strong>Michael:</strong> Yeah, it is rarely used, but you need to know that to be able to improve on it. The last difference is that echo is way faster than the print statement.</br>\n<strong>Susanne:</strong> Okay, let's move to the print statement. I'm going after you with all this PHP learning.</br>", "time": "41000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n6le02ob04re2img03.jpg", "text": "<strong>Michael:</strong> Well, the print statement has a stated value of 1, so you can use it in different expressions. In addition, while print statement is not as fast as the echo statement, it can take on arguments. That's it.</br>\n<strong>Susanne:</strong> Thanks for all the explanation. The differences are pretty small. I think I'm going to stick to the echo statement. It seems more useful.</br>\n<strong>Michael:</strong> That's a good idea, but it's important to improve upon these two concepts because I just gave you general information about them.", "time": "76000"}]}, "type": "slideshow"}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "type": "WLC", "questions": [], "stimuli_ids": [0], "img_path": "", "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can mention the two basic ways to get output in PHP.   ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about the echo output statement.   ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can discuss ideas about the print output statement. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "Do you know how to generate data to the screen? Explain. ", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "What is one of the advantages of using statements? Explain.", "type": "Free Header Item"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partner", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": "Discussing Output Statements in PHP", "type": "free_type_text_area"}]}, {"id": 1, "template_override": 1, "type": "STI", "questions": [{"id": 0, "responses": [{"selected": 0, "id": 0, "correct": 0, "_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}]}, {"selected": 0, "id": 1, "correct": 1, "_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}]}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}