it in different expressions.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n6le02ob04aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "slight ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a small amount of somehting", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: But, they have some slight differences.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n6le02ob04aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "get at ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to reach to a specific topic or point in a conversation", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(phrasal verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I was going to get at it, but you interrupted me.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n6le02ob04aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "echo statement", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a command used to output data", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The echo statement cannot return value. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n6le02ob04aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "stick to ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to follow, or to keep doing something as always", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(phrasal verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I think I'm going to stick to the echo statement. It seems more useful.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n6le02ob04aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Go over the features of Echo and Print in PHP. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write down the things that make them different. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Design a comparative chart which represents the differences and similitudes between echo and print statements. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Exchange your chart with a partner/instructor and formulate some negative questions.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I identify the two basic ways to get output in PHP. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I recognize differences and similitudes between echo and print statement. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I mention the characteristics of echo and print statement. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I design a comparative chart that represents the information above.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Phrasal Verbs", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<b>Phrasal verbs </b> are verbs compound of two words, which have a different meaning than usual.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "It's important to keep in mind that a <b>phrasal verb</b> is made up of two words, a verb and a preposition or an adverb. Look at the following structure: <center><br> <br> Subject + <b>verb </b>+ <b>preposition/adverb</b> + complement</center><br><br> There are many phrasal verbs, but in this lesson, we will focus on three.</br></br>  <strong>Get at</strong> means to reach to a specific topic or point in a conversation.<br> Example: <i> <b><br> A:</b> I don't understand your point.</br> <b>B:</b> Let me <b>get at</b> the conclusion; then, you'll see that everything makes sense.</i> <br> <br> <strong>Go after</strong> means to follow a dream or goal.</br> Example: <i><br> After finishing my major, I'm <b>going after</b> a job in that famous company of software. <br> <br> <strong>Improve on/upon</strong> means to become better at something.</br>  Examples: <br>I think you need to <b>improve on </b>your programming skills.<br>You need to <b>improve upon</b> the way you execute the codes because there are a lot of techical failures. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I was going to get at it, but you interrupted me!  ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Yeah, it is rarely used, but you need to know that to be able to improve on it .", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Negative Questions to Ask for Agreement", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use <b> negative questions </b> when you want to confirm an action, an activity or an event. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "In English, it is common to make questions in a <b>negative form </b>to look for the agreement of the person we are speaking</br></br>  Examples </br><i> A: <b>Don't</b> you think we should divide this work accordingly?</br> B: I think you are right. </br></br>  A: Carol, <b>didn't</b> you go to the cinemas last night?</br> B: Yeah, I did. Why? </i>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Hey Michael, don't you think that the echo and print statement are basically the same? ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "The echo statement has no return value, I think that's the biggest difference, don't you agree?", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}]}